\section{Discussion}

An interesting proposition here is considering whether or not voters in low-salience 
and low-turnout school board elections are a subset of the voters in higher salience 
and higher turnout elections \citep{Cho2008}.

Table \ref{tab:lagmods} and \ref{tab:meTurnout} both provide strong evidence that 
in Wisconsin spring elections voters are habit forming. The electorate in these 
elections is smaller and highly autocorrelated. As the electorate size increases, 
the less prior turnout matters. This is exactly in line with the theoretical 
expectation that smaller electorates are more habit forming than larger electorates. 
Though school boards are experiencing much less voter turnout, they appear to be 
behaving within the expectations of other electorates.

Table \ref{tab:closemods} and \ref{tab:meClose} competitive races matter for turnout in school board elections as well. 
Uncontested races have lower voter turnout than races with contestation. Little 
difference exists between races for open seats and races featuring an incumbent. 
Competitiveness matters as well, with races where a non-competitive candidate is 
running seeing voter turnout depressed as well. (substantive interpretation). 

A school board specific effects were explored in Table \ref{tab:teachmods} and 
\ref{tab:meInf}. In this, the larger the teacher share of voters in the district, 
the lower the voter turnout. This is in line with the theory of regulatory capture -- 
the nomination process being strongly controlled by the teacher union. 

- Voters appear to be habit forming
- Election closeness matters, but more work to do
- Union participation downplays turnout

